      <!-- 4. Research Experience -->
      <section class="cv-section" id="research-experience" aria-labelledby="research-title">
        <h2 id="research-title">Research Experience</h2>
        <article class="entry">
          <div class="entry-heading">
            <h3>Graduate Research Assistant</h3>
            <span class="entry-date">Sep 2025 - Present</span>
          </div>
          <p class="entry-meta">KAIST &middot; Daejeon, Korea</p>
          <p>
            Investigating orbital edge computing architectures that combine conventional
            LEO mega-constellations with dawn-dusk sun-synchronous orbit (DDSSO) nodes
            and heterogeneous onboard GPUs to address power and computational bottlenecks
            from an astrodynamics and space-systems perspective.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li>
              Developing a multidisciplinary design optimization framework that links spacecraft
              volume, solar-array size, orbit-dependent instantaneous and average solar power
              generation, and onboard GPU capability; evaluating whether higher-performance
              computing on DDSSO nodes can provide a better TSR-CAPEX tradeoff than expanding
              a LEO-only constellation. (Current)
            </li>
            <li>
              Formulated a system model and proposed an evaluation metric for the DDSSO-assisted
              LEO space data center; designed and propagated LEO and DDSSO orbits in MATLAB and
              quantified QoS gains over a conventional LEO-only constellation while evaluating
              the effects of DDSSO altitude and onboard computing capability; paper submitted to KICS.
            </li>
            <li>
              Proposed a DDSSO-assisted LEO architecture that extends satellites beyond conventional
              bent-pipes to orbital edge computing and space data center nodes; leveraged the
              near-continuous solar illumination of the DDSSO plane to support higher onboard
              computing capability and submitted a position paper to KICS.
            </li>
            <li>
              Developed a numerical two-body orbit propagator in MATLAB using ODE45. Verified
              the conservation of orbital quantities and cross-checked propagated states against
              an independent Keplerian propagator and GMAT's RK89 integrator across different orbit
              cases; extended the framework to include J2 perturbation and am currently developing
              a TudatPy-based orbit propagation and solar-power generation framework to evaluate
              orbit-dependent power availability.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </article>
